c inclination Examination of Balance Gait and External aids/assistive Device used etc • **Sensory Examination** Superficial and Deep sensation Reflexes Dermatomes and Myotomes etc • **Dermatomes** • **Definition** Areas of skin supplied by sensory fibers from a single spinal nerve root • **Purpose** Helps identify sensory loss due to nerve root lesions • **Examples** • C6 Thumb • C7 Middle finger • C8 Little finger • L4 Medial side of leg • L5 Dorsum of foot and great toe • S1 Lateral side of foot • **Normal** Sensation is intact in the tested dermatome • **5 Myotomes** • **Definition** Groups of muscles predominantly supplied by a single spinal nerve root • **Purpose** Assesses motor function of spinal nerve roots • **Examples** • C5 Shoulder abduction • C6 Wrist extension • C7 Elbow extension • C8 Finger flexion • L2 Hip flexion • L3 Knee extension • L4 Ankle dorsiflexion • L5 Great toe extension • S1 Ankle plantarflexion • **Normal** Full strength usually 5/5 on MMT --- **Assessment of Hand Function** • **1 Hand Pinches** • **Definition** Holding an object between the thumb and fingers • **Types** Tip to tip lateral pinch three jaw chuck pinch • **Assessment** Pinch gauge • **Example** Holding a key or picking up a coin • **2 Hand Grip** • **Definition** Ability to grasp and hold objects using the hand • **Types** Power grip and precision grip • **Assessment** Hand dynamometer • **Normal** Adequate grip strength for daily activities • **Example** Holding a bottle or squeezing a ball • **3 Routine Sensory Motor Evaluation** • **Definition** Assessment of sensory and motor functions of the hand • **Motor assessment** Muscle strength grip pinch thumb opposition and finger movements • **Sensory assessment** Light touch pain temperature vibration and joint position sense • **Normal** Intact sensation normal strength and coordinated movements • **Example** Weak thumb opposition may indicate median nerve involvement • **4 Stereognosis** • **Definition** Ability to identify an object by touch without seeing it • **Assessment** Patient identifies an object with eyes closed • **Normal** Correct identification of objects • **Example** Identifying a coin or key by touch • **Abnormal** Inability to recognize an object by touch is called astereognosis --- ////// **Cardiovascular and Pulmonary dysfunction** • Symmetry/Deformities of Head Neck Extremities Trophic changes • Examination of cough sputum tactile and vocal fremitus mobility of thoracic spine and rib cage percussion etc • **1 Auscultation** • **Definition** Listening to internal body sounds using a stethoscope • **Normal heart sounds** • S1 Closure of mitral and tricuspid valves heard as lub • S2 Closure of aortic and pulmonary valves heard as dub • **Normal heart rate** 60 to 100 beats/minute in adults • **Abnormal heart sounds** • Murmur Abnormal sound due to turbulent blood flow • S3 May be normal in young adults but can indicate heart failure • S4 May occur with reduced ventricular compliance • **Normal breath sounds** • Vesicular Soft low pitched sounds over most lung fields • Bronchial Heard normally over the trachea • Bronchovesicular Heard near the major bronchi • **Abnormal breath sounds** • Crackles Popping sounds eg pulmonary edema or pneumonia • Wheeze Musical sound eg asthma • Rhonchi Low pitched sounds due to airway secretions • Stridor Harsh sound due to upper airway obstruction • **Reduced or absent breath sounds** May occur with pneumothorax or pleural effusion • **2 Chest Expansion** • **Definition** Measurement of the increase in chest circumference during inspiration • **Method** Measure chest circumference during maximal expiration and inspiration using a measuring tape • **Common sites** Axillary nipple line and xiphisternal level • **Normal** Approximately 3 to 5 cm in healthy adults depending on age and measurement site • **Example** Reduced chest expansion in COPD • **3 Symmetry of Chest Movement** • **Definition** Assessment of equal movement of both sides of the chest during breathing • **Method** Observe chest movement or place hands symmetrically over the chest • **Normal** Both sides expand equally during inspiration • **Abnormal** Unequal chest expansion • **Example** Reduced movement on one side in pleural effusion or pneumothorax • **4 Body Mass Index BMI** • **Definition** Measurement of body weight in relation to height • **Formula** BMI equals Weight kg divided by Height m2 • **Normal adult BMI WHO** 18 point 5 to 24 point 9 kg/m2 • **Example** Weight 60 kg height 1 point 65 m BMI equals 22 point 0 kg/m2 • **5 Waist Hip Ratio WHR** • **Definition** Ratio of waist circumference to hip circumference • **Formula** WHR equals Waist circumference divided by Hip circumference • **Normal reference values WHO** • Men less than or equal to 0 point 90 • Women less than or equal to 0 point 85 • **Example** Waist 80 cm hip 100 cm WHR equals 0 point 80 • **Purpose** Assesses abdominal fat distribution and associated health risk","thinking":"**Prioritizing Specific Tools**\n\nI'm focusing on the importance of selecting the most appropriate tool for each task. The goal is to maximize efficiency. I am making an active effort to ensure that I always choose the correct approach to each problem or challenge, such as choosing a relevant 'view_file' utility where possible.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Usage**\n\nI'm now deeply immersed in refining tool selection based on several firm guidelines. • I'm meticulously avoiding `cat` for file creation/appending, and I'm always favoring `grep_search` where appropriate. • I'm making a conscious effort to avoid `ls`, `cat`, `grep`, and `sed` for their respective primary purposes. • I'm also remembering to recall the critical instructions 1 and 2 before making any tool calls.\n\n\n**Analyzing Separators' Role**\n\nI've got it, the user wants me to replace line breaks with bullet points! They included a semicolon and a bullet in their example, clarifying the use of the `•` separator. I'm focu