arrying a heavy object of 10 pounds (4.5 kg)\n• Removing something from your back pocket\n\n• Scoring Methodology and Calculation\n• Scored using either a Visual Analogue Scale (VAS, 0 to 10 cm) or a Numeric Rating Scale (NRS, 0 to 10).\n• 0 indicates \"no pain / no difficulty\"; 10 indicates \"worst pain imaginable / so difficult that help was required.\"\n• Subscale calculations:\n• Pain Score (%) = (Sum of pain subscale items / 50) x 100\n• Disability Score (%) = (Sum of disability subscale items / 80) x 100\n• Total SPADI Score:\n• Total SPADI (%) = (Sum of all 13 item scores / 130) x 100\n• If items are skipped/omitted:\n• Total SPADI (%) = (Total points scored / (10 x Number of items answered)) x 100\n\n• Score Interpretation\n• 0%: Best score, indicating completely normal function without shoulder pain.\n• 100%: Worst score, indicating complete functional loss and severe, intractable pain.\n• A reduction in percentage indicates clinical functional improvement.\n\n• Relevance in Physiotherapy Practice\n• Allows the therapist to distinguish whether a patient's primary impairment is pain-dominant (acute stage) or disability-dominant (chronic or adhesive stage), directly modifying treatment choices (e.g., modalities vs. aggressive mobilization).\n• Takes less than 5 minutes for the patient to complete, making it ideal for busy outpatient clinics.\n\n• Clinical Limitations\n• Does not address high-level athletic performance, throwing mechanics, or overhead sports participation.\n• Focuses solely on unilateral symptoms; less sensitive if bilateral shoulder dysfunction exists.\n\n3. Disabilities of the Arm, Shoulder and Hand (DASH)\n• Definition and Overview\n• Developed in 1996 jointly by the Institute for Work & Health (IWH) and the American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons (AAOS).\n• A comprehensive 30-item, upper-extremity-specific regional outcome tool designed to measure physical disability and symptoms across any single joint or combination of joints along the entire upper extremity kinetic chain (shoulder, elbow, wrist, and hand).\n\n• Structure and Domains\n• Comprises 30 mandatory core items covering three primary clinical domains:\n• Degree of physical difficulty in performing gross and fine motor tasks (21 items, such as opening a tight jar, writing, turning a key, preparing a meal, pushing open a heavy door, carrying a briefcase, washing back, changing a lightbulb).\n• Severity of clinical symptoms (5 items assessing pain intensity, activity-related pain, tingling/pins and needles, weakness, and joint stiffness).\n• Psychosocial and occupational impact (4 items assessing social activities, work performance, sleep disturbance, and emotional impact/confidence).\n• Optional Modules (4 items each):\n• Work Module (for workers whose upper limb issues impact heavy or repetitive tasks).\n• Sports / Performing Arts Module (for athletes, musicians, and performers).\n\n• Scoring System and Formula\n• Each of the 30 items is scored on a 5-point Likert scale:\n• 1 = No difficulty / No symptom\n• 2 = Mild difficulty / Mild symptom\n• 3 = Moderate difficulty / Moderate symptom\n• 4 = Severe difficulty / Severe symptom\n• 5 = Unable to do / Extreme symptom\n• Rule for missing data: At least 27 of the 30 items (90%) must be completed to compute a valid score.\n• Formula for Core DASH:\n• DASH Score = [((Sum of n responses) / n) - 1] x 25\n• Where n is the number of completed items.\n• Optional Modules Scoring:\n• Scored independently using an identical mathematical formula to produce a separate score out of 100.\n\n• Score Interpretation\n• 0 = No disability (optimal upper limb performance).\n• 100 = Maximum upper limb disability and severe symptoms.\n• A lower score reflects improved clinical status.\n\n• Relevance in Physiotherapy Practice\n• Captures kinetic chain dysfunction (e.g., how a wrist fracture alters shoulder kinematics and daily upper-limb capacity).\n• Enables standardized comparison between different upper quarter diagnoses under the same rehabilitation program.\n\n• Clinical Limitations\n• The 30-item core questionnaire can be time-consuming for elderly or fatigued patients (which led to the creation of the 11-item QuickDASH).\n• Does not discriminate which specific anatomical joint of the arm is the primary pain generator.\n\n4. Oswestry Disability Index (ODI)\n• Definition and Overview\n• Developed by John O’Brien and Jeremy Fairbank in 1980 (often referred to as the Oswestry Low Back Pain Questionnaire).\n• Widely accepted as the international \"gold standard\" condition-specific patient-reported outcome measure for determining permanent or functional disability in acute and chronic low back pain (LBP), lumbar disc herniations, spinal canal stenosis, and post-lumbar fusion surgeries.\n\n• Structure and Clinical Domains\n• Composed of 10 clinical sections, each addressing a critical daily life domain impacted by lumbar pathology:\n• Section 1: Pain Intensity\n• Section 2: Personal Care (washing, dressing)\n• Section 3: Lifting\n• Section 4: Walking\n• Section 5: Sitting\n• Section 6: Standing\n• Section 7: Sleeping\n• Section 8: Sex Life (if applicable/acceptable to patient)\n• Section 9: Social Life\n• Section 10: Travelling\n\n• Scoring Methodology and Calculation\n• Each section contains 6 statements scored from 0 to 5:\n• 0 indicates the least limitation or no pain.\n• 5 indicates the greatest functional restriction or maximum pain.\n• Maximum possible raw score = 50 (if all 10 sections are completed).\n• Calculation when all sections are answered:\n• ODI Percentage (%) = (Total Raw Score / 50) x 100\n• Calculation when Section 8 (Sex Life) is omitted:\n• ODI Percentage (%) = (Total Raw Score / 45) x 100\n\n• Score Interpretation and Clinical Grading\n• 0% to 20% (Minimal Disability): The patient can cope with most living activities. Treatment usually centers on ergonomics, core stabilization, and general fitness.\n• 21% to 40% (Moderate Disability): The patient experiences pain with sitting, lifting, and standing. Conservative management (manual therapy, electrotherapy, active directional preference exercises) is indicated.\n• 41% to 60% (Severe Disability): Pain remains the main problem; activities of daily living are significantly disrupted. Requires detailed diagnostic evaluation and structured intervention.\n• 61% to 80% (Crippled): Back pain impinges on all aspects of the patient's life. Intervention requires advanced pain management, multidisciplinary care, or surgical consultation.\n• 81% to 100% (Bedbound or Symptom Exaggeration): These patients are either completely bedridden or exaggerating their symptoms (requires screening for non-organic signs/Waddell signs).\n\n• Relevance in Physiotherapy Practice\n• Establishes functional baseline and tracks progress over weeks of lumbar core strengthening, McKenzie extension, or motor control stabilization.\n• Serves as standard legal and disability documentation for return-to-work evaluations and medicolegal reporting.\n\n• Clinical Limitations\n• Patients from conservative cultures frequently leave the \"Sex Life\" section blank, necessitating manual formula adjustments.\n• Shows\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-17T23:33:24+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
