{"step_index":22,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T18:12:02Z","thinking":"**Prioritizing Specific Tools**\n\nI'm focusing on the importance of selecting the most appropriate tool for each task. The goal is to maximize efficiency. I am making an active effort to ensure that I always choose the correct approach to each problem or challenge, such as choosing a relevant 'view_file' utility where possible.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Usage**\n\nI'm now implementing strict tool selection rules, especially avoiding `cat`, `grep` within bash, and generic tools for specific tasks. I recall the critical instruction to list all relevant tools beforehand. The `view_file` tool failed with `.docx`, so I'm writing a Python script to extract and view the relevant XML data, leveraging the `zipfile` module directly. This approach bypasses the limitations encountered earlier.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"python -c \"import zipfile, xml.etree.ElementTree as ET; docx_path = r'C:\\Users\\renu5\\Downloads\\15.docx'; docx = zipfile.ZipFile(docx_path); tree = ET.XML(docx.read('word/document.xml')); ns = {'w': 'http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/wordprocessingml/2006/main'}; text = []; [text.append(''.join([node.text for node in p.findall('.//w:t', ns) if node.text])) for p in tree.findall('.//w:p', ns) if p.findall('.//w:t', ns)]; print('\\n'.join(text))\"","Cwd":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Extracting text from docx","toolSummary":"Extract text"}}]}
{"step_index":23,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T18:12:17Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-17T23:42:17+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-17T23:42:20+05:30\n\nThe command exited with code 0.\nOutput:\n<truncated 1 lines>\nTransradial Route: Patient may ambulate within 1 to 2 hours; avoid wrist flexion, weight-bearing through the hand, and lifting objects over 2 to 3 kg for 48 to 72 hours.\r\nInspect the puncture site for active bleeding, expanding swelling/hematoma, or distal pulse loss (dorsalis pedis/radial artery) before initiating mobilization.\r\n7. Chest X-Ray (CXR)\r\nDefinition\r\nA chest radiograph is a non-invasive projection transmission image displaying the differential radiodensity of thoracic tissues: air (black/radiolucent), fat (dark gray), water/soft tissue (light gray), and bone/metal (white/radiopaque).\r\nStandard Projections\r\nPosteroanterior (PA) View: Gold standard; taken in full upright inspiration with X-ray beam travelling from back to front; minimizes cardiac magnification.\r\nAnteroposterior (AP) View: Performed at bedside on immobilized or ICU patients; magnifies cardiac silhouette size and limits scapular clearance.\r\nSystematic Evaluation Approach (ABCDE Method)\r\nA - Airway: Trachea position (normally midline, vertically oriented over spinous processes).\r\nB - Breathing / Lung Fields: Vascular markings, lucency, symmetric expansion, pleural spaces.\r\nC - Cardiac Silhouette & Mediastinum: Cardiothoracic Ratio (CTR) on PA view is normally less than 0.50 (50%); mediastinum shape and borders.\r\nD - Diaphragm: Both hemidiaphragms rounded, smooth, right sitting 1 to 2 cm higher than left; costophrenic and cardiophrenic angles sharp and clear.\r\nE - Everything Else / Skeleton: Ribs, clavicles, humeri, subcutaneous tissues, indwelling tubes, catheters, pacing wires.\r\nCommon Pathological Variations on CXR\r\nLobular Consolidation (Pneumonia):\r\nHomogeneous radio-opacity with preservation of lung volume.\r\nPresence of \"Air Bronchograms\" (branching lucent tubular airways outlined by fluid-filled alveoli).\r\nSilhouette sign: Loss of the right heart border indicates right middle lobe consolidation.\r\nPleural Effusion:\r\nBlunting of the costophrenic angle (evident when fluid exceeds 200 mL).\r\nMeniscus sign: Homogeneous dense opacity with a concave superior border sloping upward toward the lateral chest wall.\r\nMassive effusion displaces the mediastinum and trachea away from the affected side.\r\nPneumothorax:\r\nComplete absence of lung markings at the peripheral thoracic cavity.\r\nVisible sharp white visceral pleural line separated from the chest wall by radiolucent air.\r\nTension Pneumothorax: Marked tracheal and mediastinal shift toward the contralateral side with flattened/depressed ipsilateral hemidiaphragm.\r\nAtelectasis (Lung Collapse):\r\nOpacity accompanied by direct signs of volume loss: tracheal, cardiac, and mediastinal shift toward the side of collapse.\r\nCrowding of ribs and elevation of the ipsilateral hemidiaphragm.\r\nCOPD / Emphysema:\r\nBilateral pulmonary hyperinflation with increased lung field radiolucency.\r\nFlattened, depressed hemidiaphragms with widened intercostal spaces.\r\nElongated, narrow \"tubular\" heart shadow; enlarged retrosternal clear space on lateral view.\r\nPulmonary Edema (Congestive Heart Failure):\r\nCardiomegaly (CTR greater than 0.50).\r\nCephalization of pulmonary vessels (upper lobe blood diversion).\r\nPerihilar \"Bat-wing\" or \"Butterfly\" alveolar infiltrates.\r\nKerley B lines (short, horizontal peripheral lines representing thickened interlobular septa).\r\nPhysiotherapy Implications\r\nLocalizes pulmonary pathologies to specific segments, directly guiding postural drainage positioning.\r\nConsolidation/Atelectasis confirms the need for thoracic expansion techniques, chest percussion, vibration, and directed autogenic drainage.\r\nPneumothorax is a strict contraindication to positive pressure techniques (PEP, IPPB) and vigorous manual chest clapping until an intercostal chest tube (ICD) is placed and underwater seal bubbling stabilizes.\r\n8. Tests for Peripheral Arterial and Venous Conditions\r\nDefinition\r\nClinical and non-invasive diagnostic tests evaluating arterial flow velocity, lumen patency, tissue perfusion, and venous valvular competence in the lower and upper extremities.\r\nPeripheral Arterial Disease (PAD) Tests\r\nAnkle-Brachial Index (ABI):\r\nDefinition: Ratio of the highest systolic blood pressure at the ankle to the highest systolic blood pressure in the brachial arteries.\r\nProcedure: Patient rests supine for 10 minutes. A Doppler ultrasound probe (5 to 10 MHz) and pneumatic cuff measure systolic blood pressure in both brachial arteries and bilateral ankle arteries (Dorsalis Pedis and Posterior Tibial).\r\nCalculation: \\text{ABI} = \\frac{\\text{Highest Ankle Systolic Pressure (DP or PT)}}{\\text{Highest Brachial Systolic Pressure (Right or Left)}}\r\nClinical Values and Interpretation:\r\n1.00 to 1.40: Normal arterial perfusion.\r\n0.91 to 0.99: Borderline arterial disease.\r\n0.41 to 0.90: Mild-to-moderate PAD (characteristic intermittent claudication during walking).\r\nLess than or equal to 0.40: Severe PAD (critical limb ischemia, rest pain, gangrene risk).\r\nGreater than 1.40: Non-compressible, calcified rigid arteries (commonly seen in diabetes mellitus; requires Toe-Brachial Index).\r\nBuerger�s Test (Postural Color Changes):\r\nThe patient lies supine; the examiner elevates the lower extremity to 45 to 60 degrees for 1 to 2 minutes.\r\nAbnormal: Development of marked pallor in less than 60 seconds (positive vascular angle of Buerger less than 20 degrees indicates severe ischemia).\r\nOn returning the leg to a dependent position, a delay in pink reperfusion greater than 15 seconds followed by a dark purplish-red discoloration (Rubor of Dependency) confirms chronic arterial insufficiency.\r\nCapillary Refill Time (CRT):\r\nFirm pressure applied to the distal digital pulp/nail bed for 5 seconds until blanched.\r\nNormal: Color returns in less than 2 seconds; prolonged CRT indicates distal hypoperfusion.\r\nPeripheral Venous Disease Tests\r\nWells Clinical Score for Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT):\r\nStratifies pre-test probability of DVT based on risk factors: active cancer (+1), paralysis/recent plaster immobilization (+1), bedridden over 3 days or major surgery within 12 weeks (+1), localized tenderness along deep venous system (+1), entire leg swollen (+1), calf swelling greater than 3 cm compared to asymptomatic side (+1), pitting edema confined to symptomatic leg (+1), collateral superficial non-varicose veins (+1), alternative diagnosis as likely as DVT (-2).\r\nScore greater than or equal to 2: DVT likely (urgent Duplex Ultrasound required).\r\nHoman�s Sign:\r\nPassive dorsiflexion of the ankle with the knee extended to elicit calf pain.\r\nClinical Note: Insensitive, non-specific, and carries a mechanical risk of dislodging an unstable clot. It is no longer recommended in modern clinical guidelines.\r\nTrendelenburg Test (Brodie-Trendelenburg Test):\r\nDifferentiates saphenofemoral junction incompetence from deep/perforator valvular incompetence in varicose veins.\r\nLeg is elevated to empty superficial veins; a tourniquet is applied around the upper thigh.\r\nPatient stands: rapid retrograde filling from above before releasing the tourniquet indicates perforator incompetence; rapid filling from above after tourniquet release confirms saphenofemoral junction valve incompetence.\r\nVenous Duplex Ultrasound:\r\nGold standard non-invasive diagnostic investigation for DVT; evaluates venous compressibility and venous flow patterns.\r\nPhysiotherapy Implications and Precautions\r\nIn Intermittent Claudication (PAD with ABI 0.5 to 0.9): Supervised treadmill walking is the primary conservative therapy. Exercise to moderate-to-strong claudication pain (grade 3 on 4-point scale), rest until pain resolves, and repeat for 30 to 45 minutes, 3 to 5 times weekly.\r\nIn Severe PAD (ABI less than 0.5): High-intensity exercise, leg elevation, and external compression bandages are strictly contraindicated.\r\nIn Acute Confirmed DVT: Hold all ambulation, range of motion exercises, and calf massage until adequate therapeutic anticoagulation is established (typically 24 to 48 hours with Low Molecular Weight Heparin).\r\nIn Chronic Venous Insufficiency: Promote leg elevation above heart level, prescribe graduated compression stockings (20 to 30 mmHg), and prescribe active calf muscle pump exercises./////////\r\n\n"}
