{"step_index":27,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T18:12:39Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-17T23:42:39+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-17T23:42:39+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/15.txt`\nTotal Lines: 247\nTotal Bytes: 24639\nShowing lines 1 to 247\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: 1. Blood Investigations and Cardiac Biomarkers\r\n2: Definition\r\n3: Blood investigations and biomarkers are laboratory tests performed on venous or arterial blood samples to evaluate organ function, systemic inflammation, hematological balance, and myocardial cellular damage or heart failure.\r\n4: Key Cardiac and Hematological Markers\r\n5: Cardiac Troponins (cTnI and cTnT):\r\n6: Gold standard regulatory contractile proteins released exclusively upon cardiomyocyte necrosis.\r\n7: Normal Value: cTnI is less than 0.04 ng/mL; high-sensitivity troponin (hs-cTn) is less than 14 ng/L.\r\n8: Timeline: Rises within 3 to 4 hours post-myocardial injury, peaks at 18 to 24 hours, and remains elevated for 10 to 14 days.\r\n9: Clinical Significance: Confirms Acute Myocardial Infarction (AMI); determines the extent of myocardial necrosis.\r\n10: Creatine Kinase-MB (CK-MB):\r\n11: Isoenzyme localized primarily in cardiac muscle tissue.\r\n12: Normal Value: Less than 5% of total CK or 0 to 5 ng/mL.\r\n13: Timeline: Rises within 4 to 6 hours, peaks at 18 to 24 hours, and normalizes within 48 to 72 hours.\r\n14: Clinical Significance: Useful for detecting early re-infarction because it clears much faster than troponin.\r\n15: B-Type Natriuretic Peptide (BNP and NT-proBNP):\r\n16: Neurohormone secreted by ventricular myocytes in response to increased ventricular wall stretch and volume overload.\r\n17: Normal Value: BNP is less than 100 pg/mL; NT-proBNP is less than 300 pg/mL.\r\n18: Clinical Significance: Distinguishes dyspnea caused by congestive heart failure (elevated levels) from dyspnea of primary pulmonary origin.\r\n19: Complete Blood Count (CBC) and Hemoglobin (Hb):\r\n20: Normal Hemoglobin: 13 to 17 g/dL for males; 12 to 15 g/dL for females.\r\n21: Clinical Significance: Anemia (Hb less than 8 g/dL) drastically impairs peripheral oxygen delivery, causing resting tachycardia, fatigue, and reduced exercise tolerance.\r\n22: Inflammatory Markers (CRP and ESR):\r\n23: High-Sensitivity C-Reactive Protein (hs-CRP): Values greater than 3.0 mg/L reflect systemic inflammation and elevated atherothrombotic risk.\r\n24: Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate (ESR): Elevated in pericarditis, infective endocarditis, and chronic pulmonary infections.\r\n25: Coagulation Profile (D-Dimer, PT, INR):\r\n26: D-Dimer: Normal is less than 500 ng/mL; elevated levels indicate active fibrinolysis and raise suspicion for deep vein thrombosis (DVT) or pulmonary embolism (PE).\r\n27: International Normalized Ratio (INR): Normal is 0.8 to 1.2; therapeutic range for anticoagulated patients is 2.0 to 3.0.\r\n28: Physiotherapy Implications and Exercise Precautions\r\n29: Active myocardial infarction with progressively rising troponin levels is an absolute contraindication to physical therapy exercise.\r\n30: Ambulation or phase I cardiac rehabilitation is deferred until cardiac enzymes peak and decline, and the patient remains hemodynamically stable for 24 hours.\r\n31: If Hemoglobin is less than 8 g/dL, postpone active resistance training; limit interventions to gentle bedside bed-mobility or assisted transfers.\r\n32: If INR is greater than 3.5, avoid vigorous chest physiotherapy, manual percussions, aggressive stretching, or high-impact mobilizations due to internal bleeding and hematoma risk.\r\n33: Severely elevated BNP indicates decompensated heart failure; fluid overload requires avoiding head-down postural drainage and minimizing cardiovascular exertion.\r\n34: 2. Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) Analysis\r\n35: Definition\r\n36: ABG analysis is an invasive diagnostic measurement of arterial blood assessing systemic oxygenation, alveolar ventilation, and metabolic or respiratory acid-base balance.\r\n37: Standard Arterial Parameters and Reference Ranges\r\n38: pH: 7.35 to 7.45 (indicates systemic hydrogen ion concentration).\r\n39: PaCO2 (Partial pressure of arterial carbon dioxide): 35 to 45 mmHg (respiratory/ventilatory component).\r\n40: PaO2 (Partial pressure of arterial oxygen): 80 to 100 mmHg on room air (arterial oxygenation status).\r\n41: HCO3- (Bicarbonate concentration): 22 to 26 mEq/L (metabolic/renal component).\r\n42: Base Excess (BE): -2 to +2 mEq/L (amount of buffer required to restore normal pH).\r\n43: SaO2 (Arterial oxygen saturation): 95% to 100%.\r\n44: Systematic Steps for ABG Interpretation\r\n45: Step 1 (Check pH): A pH less than 7.35 indicates acidemia; a pH greater than 7.45 indicates alkalemia.\r\n46: Step 2 (Check PaCO2): If altered in the direction opposite to pH (low pH with high PaCO2), the primary disorder is respiratory.\r\n47: Step 3 (Check HCO3-): If altered in the same direction as pH (low pH with low HCO3-), the primary disorder is metabolic.\r\n48: Step 4 (Determine Compensation):\r\n49: Uncompensated: Abnormal pH, one component abnormal, other component normal.\r\n50: Partially Compensated: Abnormal pH, both PaCO2 and HCO3- abnormal in the same compensatory direction.\r\n51: Fully Compensated: Normal pH (within 7.35 to 7.45), but both PaCO2 and HCO3- remain abnormal.\r\n52: Common Pathological Patterns\r\n53: Respiratory Acidosis (pH less than 7.35, PaCO2 greater than 45 mmHg):\r\n54: Causes: Alveolar hypoventilation, severe COPD, respiratory muscle fatigue, central nervous system depression, Guillain-Barré syndrome.\r\n55: Respiratory Alkalosis (pH greater than 7.45, PaCO2 less than 35 mmHg):\r\n56: Causes: Hyperventilation secondary to anxiety, hypoxemia, acute pulmonary embolism, severe pain.\r\n57: Metabolic Acidosis (pH less than 7.35, HCO3- less than 22 mEq/L):\r\n58: Causes: Diabetic ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis from septic or cardiogenic shock, chronic renal failure.\r\n59: Metabolic Alkalosis (pH greater than 7.45, HCO3- greater than 26 mEq/L):\r\n60: Causes: Prolonged vomiting, high-dose diuretic administration, severe hypokalemia.\r\n61: Physiotherapy Significance\r\n62: Identifies type I respiratory failure (hypoxemic: PaO2 less than 60 mmHg with normal/low PaCO2) vs. type II respiratory failure (hypercapnic: PaCO2 greater than 50 mmHg with acidosis).\r\n63: Guides non-invasive ventilation (BiPAP) and aggressive airway clearance techniques for hypercapnic retained secretions in COPD.\r\n64: Prevents over-oxygenation in retainers: uncontrolled high-flow oxygen suppresses the hypoxic drive to breathe in chronic type II respiratory failure.\r\n65: Serves as an objective parameter for monitoring tolerance during ventilator weaning and progressive bedside mobilization.\r\n66: 3. Pulmonary Function Testing (PFT)\r\n67: Definition\r\n68: Pulmonary Function Testing encompasses non-invasive physiological evaluations—primarily spirometry, plethysmography, and gas diffusion capacity—used to quantify static and dynamic lung volumes, airway mechanics, and gas exchange efficiency.\r\n69: Key Spirometric Parameters\r\n70: Tidal Volume (TV): Normal volume inspired or expired during quiet breathing (~500 mL).\r\n71: Vital Capacity (VC) / Forced Vital Capacity (FVC): Maximum volume of air exhaled